1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:02,220
The Peace Prize
went to Navi Pillay.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:04,260
The committee names two things:

3
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:05,960
peace and international law.

4
00:00:06,290 --> 00:00:07,630
Left, the courtroom.

5
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:10,107
Right, the field,
where conflicts end.

6
00:00:11,197 --> 00:00:13,417
Born in Durban in 1941,

7
00:00:13,637 --> 00:00:16,517
she opened her own
law firm in 1967.

8
00:00:17,032 --> 00:00:18,502
The committee cites her early

9
00:00:18,502 --> 00:00:20,280
work defending Nelson Mandela.

10
00:00:21,461 --> 00:00:24,461
In 1998, at the Rwanda tribunal,

11
00:00:24,461 --> 00:00:26,581
she was one of three
judges who found that

12
00:00:26,581 --> 00:00:29,141
rape and sexual violence
can be genocide.

13
00:00:29,521 --> 00:00:31,141
The committee says her influence

14
00:00:31,141 --> 00:00:32,453
was a significant factor.

15
00:00:33,633 --> 00:00:36,713
In 2003 she presided
over the Media case:

16
00:00:37,013 --> 00:00:39,023
three men tied to
a radio station,

17
00:00:39,370 --> 00:00:40,763
a party and a newspaper.

18
00:00:41,353 --> 00:00:43,173
All three were
convicted of inciting

19
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:44,971
genocide; on appeal,

20
00:00:45,111 --> 00:00:50,002
several convictions were reversed;
the sentences became 30 to 35 years.

21
00:00:51,201 --> 00:00:53,297
On the right,
Uppsala's data record

22
00:00:53,370 --> 00:00:55,711
five hundred and seven
conflict endings,

23
00:00:55,911 --> 00:00:58,161
from 1946 to 2023.

24
00:00:58,751 --> 00:01:00,276
One in four ended in a peace

25
00:01:00,331 --> 00:01:01,761
agreement or a ceasefire.

26
00:01:02,251 --> 00:01:03,921
More of them now
simply fade out.

27
00:01:04,381 --> 00:01:07,090
Yet among clear
outcomes, since 1990,

28
00:01:07,183 --> 00:01:09,098
most are agreements
or ceasefires.

29
00:01:10,288 --> 00:01:12,108
Then came the
International Criminal

30
00:01:12,108 --> 00:01:15,020
Court, and six years
as United Nations

31
00:01:15,102 --> 00:01:16,872
High Commissioner
for Human Rights.

32
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:21,110
[Navi Pillay, 2013]
That rights are indivisible.

33
00:01:21,110 --> 00:01:23,760
We need civil and political rights

34
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:27,370
as much as we need economic,
social and cultural rights.

35
00:01:28,611 --> 00:01:30,403
Uppsala also lists three hundred

36
00:01:30,438 --> 00:01:32,240
and seventy-four
peace agreements,

37
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:35,180
signed from 1975 to 2021.

38
00:01:35,573 --> 00:01:37,393
They come from
seventy-two groups of

39
00:01:37,393 --> 00:01:39,477
linked conflicts,
so they aren't three

40
00:01:39,529 --> 00:01:41,650
hundred and seventy-four
separate tests.

41
00:01:42,221 --> 00:01:44,151
Where the fighting
went quiet, about

42
00:01:44,204 --> 00:01:46,330
half were still quiet
five years later.

43
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:48,260
The data can't say whether any

44
00:01:48,260 --> 00:01:49,960
type of agreement causes peace.

45
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:53,086
In the committee's
words, the greatest

46
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:55,516
powers have often
evaded responsibility.

47
00:01:56,786 --> 00:01:58,827
Since 2019 she has sat at the

48
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:00,536
International Court of Justice,

49
00:02:00,936 --> 00:02:02,850
chosen as a judge for one case.

50
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:06,291
The committee writes:
the judges, those

51
00:02:06,317 --> 00:02:08,387
who guard the thin
red line between order

52
00:02:08,397 --> 00:02:10,900
and chaos, as these same judges
are sanctioned,

53
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:13,811
and their institutions are attacked.

54
00:02:15,391 --> 00:02:17,281
One room records
what courts did;

55
00:02:17,421 --> 00:02:19,271
the other, how conflicts end.

56
00:02:19,601 --> 00:02:21,471
The committee joins
them in one line:

57
00:02:22,199 --> 00:02:23,849
Peace requires justice.

58
00:02:28,388 --> 00:02:31,046
This film was made
with showtime, a local

59
00:02:31,099 --> 00:02:33,236
video studio for
your coding agent.
